# Real Claude config with the per-machine Obsidian Local REST API key. init
# copies claude/config.sample.json to ~/.claude/config.json; the key is pasted
# in there and must never land in the repo.
claude/config.json

# Real Spotify MCP config (client secret + OAuth tokens). init copies
# config/spotify-mcp/spotify-config.sample.json to ~/src/spotify-mcp-server/
# and the server writes tokens there; nothing with credentials belongs here.
config/spotify-mcp/spotify-config.json

# Belt-and-braces for shell credentials. The real ones live in
# ~/.config/env-secrets.zsh, outside this repo entirely (see
# config/zsh/env/claude.zsh) -- git has no view of that path, which is the
# actual protection. These patterns only catch a secrets file that someone
# later drops into the working tree by habit.
#
# Note what an ignore rule cannot do: claude/settings.json is TRACKED and
# ~/.claude/settings.json is a symlink to it, so anything Claude Code writes
# into its settings goes straight into git. That is exactly how three mem0 API
# keys reached a public repo. Ignoring does not apply to tracked files.
config/zsh/env/secrets.zsh
config/zsh/env/*.secret.zsh
env-secrets.zsh

# macOS resource forks. A round-trip of this repo through a FAT/exFAT stick or
# a macOS-formatted volume sprays an AppleDouble "._<name>" sidecar next to
# every file (83 of them last time) and sets the execute bit on everything it
# touches. The sidecars are pure noise; the mode damage has to be undone with
# `git ls-files -s` + chmod, since core.fileMode=false would only hide it -- and
# this repo has real scripts whose 755 bit matters.
._*
.DS_Store

# Claude Code's project-local settings for this repo. Machine-local by
# convention and rewritten by Claude Code itself (it adds permissions as they
# are granted), so tracking it would mean a diff after every session -- the
# same trap the claude/settings.json note above describes, just without the
# secrets exposure.
.claude/settings.local.json
